--### Q9 — Repeat Purchase Interval

--**Lifecycle Marketing question:** *"How long until a customer comes back? When should we send the win-back email?"*

--**Output (row-level):** `customer_id, order_id, order_date, next_order_date, days_to_next_order`

--**Output (summary):** `avg_days_to_next_order, median_days_to_next_order, p90_days_to_next_order, customers_with_repeat_order`

--**Sanity check:** `days_to_next_order >= 0` on every row. `median <= p90` in the summary.

--**Pattern note:** `lead(created_at) over (partition by customer_id order by created_at)`. Filter out the last order per customer (`next_order_date IS NULL`) from the summary — including them biases the average toward infinity. You will also find a fat cluster of near-zero intervals: customers who split one shopping session into multiple orders minutes apart. Decide whether a same-day follow-up order counts as "coming back" (hint: a win-back email is irrelevant to it), compute the summary both ways, and document the choice — this single decision moves the median by days.

with customer_order_date as (
SELECT
o.customer_id as customer_id
,o.order_id as order_id 
, (o.created_at) as order_date
,lead(o.created_at) over (PARTITION BY o.customer_id order by o.created_at) as next_order_date
,lead(o.created_at) over (PARTITION BY o.customer_id order by o.created_at) -(o.created_at) as days_to_next_order_date
from ecom.orders o)


SELECT 
COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) as customers_with_repeat_order
,avg(co.days_to_next_order_date) as avg_days_to_next_order_date
,PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (order by co.days_to_next_order_date) as median_days_to_next_order_date
,PERCENTILE_CONT(0.9) WITHIN GROUP (order by co.days_to_next_order_date) as p90_days_to_next_order_date
from customer_order_date co
WHERE next_order_date IS NOT NULL;


SELECT 
COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) as customers_with_repeat_order
,avg(co.days_to_next_order_date) as avg_days_to_next_order_date
,PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (order by co.days_to_next_order_date) as median_days_to_next_order_date
,PERCENTILE_CONT(0.9) WITHIN GROUP (order by co.days_to_next_order_date) as p90_days_to_next_order_date
from customer_order_date co
WHERE next_order_date IS NOT NULL
  AND days_to_next_order_date > 0;


-- For lifecycle marketing, I would use the second version (excluding same-day repeat orders), because multiple orders placed on the same day are likely part of the same shopping session rather than a customer returning after leaving. Using the filtered version produces a more realistic repeat purchase interval for scheduling win-back emails.
